WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.076 --> 00:00:02.140
(dramatic music)

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.520
- It's so great to kind
of like get a moment

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.810
where you felt like you could still

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.680
have like a really fun theater experience,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.410
I know it's not the way folks intended

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.620
but it was really great

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.730
to really sort of see
these characters back

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.640
and doing all this amazing stuff.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.570
But why 1984, why the '80s,
why do you think we had

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.450
to set the story in this
particular time period?

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.100
- There were a bunch of reasons,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.300
one of them was after
shooting the first movie

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.300
in 1917, World War I,
everything was gray and gloomy.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.026
To be able to move on for the next story

00:00:37.026 --> 00:00:42.026
where the environment is so
alive and so pop and colorful,

00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:44.598
just felt like super great.

00:00:44.598 --> 00:00:47.073
But on the philosophical level,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.740
the '80s were the heights of everything,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.600
of success and access.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.790
And it was the time when people

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.420
didn't think about the repercussions

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.770
of what's going to happen.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.450
And we felt like it had a
lot of resembles to nowadays.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.111
- Yeah, I think it's like
the ostentatiousness of it.

00:01:09.111 --> 00:01:09.944
If that's the word
- Exactly.

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:12.560
that I was like living for.
- Exactly.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.410
- And I can't say that
anyone is more ostentatious

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.580
than what Kristen is doing with Barbara,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.670
it is just simply amazing
and they give her so much,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.120
and I loved watching y'all go toe-to-toe,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.300
as much as I liked y'all like

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.080
sort of having those like bonding moments.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.540
But, I have to ask about the fight scenes.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.920
So what was it like (chuckles)
fighting Kristen Wiig,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.220
'cause y'all look like, I hate
to say you were having fun,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.090
but it looked like you're having fun.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.990
- We were having fun, it
was a lot of work too.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.140
Patty really made a point about having

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.230
as minimum amount of CGI as possible,

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.470
which was great, but it just meant

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.920
that we had to do a lot of
the heavy lifting ourselves.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.250
So we spent, both Kristen
and I, together and apart,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.360
we spent so much time working out

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.373
and working with the stunt teams

00:02:01.373 --> 00:02:04.020
learning the choreographies and rehearsing

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.430
and doing the wire work
and doing the water work.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.780
It was like, it was a lot.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.484
But working with Kristen
made the whole experience

00:02:13.484 --> 00:02:15.470
much more delightful.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.391
- What did you do?

00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:19.141
(dramatic music)

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.690
- I was just saying the
Gal like it's so colorful,

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.450
it's just so audacious, and
she said, That's why you guys

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.870
wanted to do it in the 80s.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.070
But I'm just curious for
you personally, like,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.320
what is your connection to that timeframe?

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.110
'Cause I think everybody
approaches a decade differently.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.180
So what is it personally identify to you?

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.740
- I came of age and, you know,

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.370
I was in junior high in the 80s,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.850
and so I, well I was in high school too.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.530
But I just remember it as,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.890
from the point of view of a teenager,

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.960
it was so much fun, and
the movies were amazing,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.850
everything was exciting, and
everything seemed possible.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.970
I couldn't wait to go travel the world

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.930
and go to New York and all that stuff.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.250
So, that's what I really
remember about it,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.460
and I was excited to
capture, was that kind of

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.260
the world where everything
seems possible still.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.840
And there really was that kind of naivety,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.160
that this way of life would
ever stop being successful.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.900
And so it was interesting
to take Wonder Woman

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.250
who's now seen World War I, World War II,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.350
Vietnam, seen all of these things

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.950
and collide her with a
time of such opulence

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.450
and excess and naivety,
when she is not naive.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.580
And so that was very
interesting as a character

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.770
and visually I just loved
that it was so different

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.880
from the first movie.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.730
I got to see my, my Wonder Woman

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.840
that I grew up loving in Lynda Carter

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.900
in the right time for her

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.650
and so I loved that about it as well.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.250
- Yeah, it definitely felt
like the Lynda Carter vibes,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.430
the colorful vibes,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.100
like all of the, it
felt like an 80s movie,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.500
like a period piece almost,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.390
like it very much felt like of that era.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.060
And I, but I do have to
ask because it now fits

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.760
in the timeline differently.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.130
Has Zack tapped you for any like

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.770
Snider cut, little nods back,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.430
like I'm just curious if he's now like,

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.700
seen it and he's gonna
put some stuff in there.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.090
- No, he hasn't, he has seen it.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.430
He's seen the film.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.263
But, I don't-
- Okay.

00:04:13.263 --> 00:04:15.450
- I haven't seen the Snider
cut yet and what he's doing

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.780
but no, he didn't he
didn't require anything

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:18.971
to put this movie off.

00:04:18.971 --> 00:04:21.200
(laughs)

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.721
- No, no, I know he doesn't need anything.

00:04:22.721 --> 00:04:24.650
Folks are just gonna be
really happy to see it.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.775
- Okay, I don't mean it
that way, you know, I just,

00:04:26.775 --> 00:04:30.420
he didn't need me to
retrospectively, yeah, know nothing.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.253
- Gotcha you.

00:04:31.253 --> 00:04:32.270
No, no, it's great, though.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.270
But it's in the canon now,
that's what's so great

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.480
about this is it sets up
this whole new timeline,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.100
so I really do love that.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.060
And then also we have to
ask about the recent news

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.350
and I just wanna know.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.370
You probably can't tell us
anything but its history,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.460
so we kinda know a little.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.450
What can tell us about Cleopatra,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.320
and why you guys are teaming
up this next time again?

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.940
- Cleopatra is such an interesting story,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.280
but it's one of those stories
that's never been told,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.220
really from her point of view.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.160
It's only been told by the Romans

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.050
who hated her and killed her.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.310
So when you really...

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.270
It's one of those, when you
start to look at the story,

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.750
you realize something very different

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.090
than you think was going on.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:14.060
And she was just an epic,
legendary, powerful leader.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.440
And so what an interesting story.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.810
Gal is such an incredible
person to play it.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.760
And so, that's such a,
it's a beautiful project.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:24.718
- You know what, I'm ready to go.

00:05:24.718 --> 00:05:25.551
(upbeat music)

00:05:25.551 --> 00:05:26.830
- I think we can do better.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.570
- Parachute pants?

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:29.403
- Yeah.

00:05:29.403 --> 00:05:31.074
- Does everybody parachute now?

00:05:31.074 --> 00:05:33.491
(rock music)

